.TH "PERFORMANCECONTAINERTIMING.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 26, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceContainerTiming.toJSON \- PerformanceContainerTiming:
toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] interface is a serializer; it
returns a JSON representation of the
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] object.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]JSON\f[R] object that is the serialization of the
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] object.
.PP
The JSON doesn\(cqt contain the \f[CR]lastPaintedElement\f[R] or
\f[CR]rootElement\f[R] properties, because they are of type
\f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R], which doesn\(cqt provide a \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R]
operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using the toJSON method
This example demonstrates how the \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method is used.
.SS HTML
First we define a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element that is marked as a
container root identified as \f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R] by its
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute.
The section contains two other elements that will be drawn, resulting in
a container performance entry.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>The parent element of this paragraph is a container root.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs each entry,
calling the \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method directly to get the JSON object.
The observer is then started, filtering only for entries of type
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      log(JSON.stringify(entry.toJSON(), \f[B]null\f[R], 2));
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.SS Result
The JSON output is displayed in the log that follows the elements.
.PP
Note that in this case only one entry is logged, even though the
container root has two child elements, because both elements paint in
the container\(cqs first frame.
The browser emits at most one \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R]
entry for every container root on each rendering frame (aggregating
every element that paints during that frame).
.PP
The entry should look something like this:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
  \(dqentryType\(dq: \(dqcontainer\(dq,
  \(dqstartTime\(dq: 77,
  \(dqduration\(dq: 0,
  \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dqhero\(dq,
  \(dqintersectionRect\(dq: {
    \(dqx\(dq: 0,
    \(dqy\(dq: 19.916666666666668,
    \(dqwidth\(dq: 359.7,
    \(dqheight\(dq: 66.71666666666667,
    \(dqtop\(dq: 19.916666666666668,
    \(dqright\(dq: 359.7,
    \(dqbottom\(dq: 86.63333333333334,
    \(dqleft\(dq: 0
  },
  \(dqsize\(dq: 10519,
  \(dqfirstRenderTime\(dq: 77,
  \(dqpaintTime\(dq: 77,
  \(dqpresentationTime\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R]
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]JSON\f[R]
